<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Labayu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century BCE</span> · Also known as Lab'ayu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Labayu was ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a> in the hill country of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> in the mid-14th century BCE, known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>. While professing loyalty to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>, he enlarged his territory at the expense of neighbouring Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, provoking complaints from the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a> and Gezer. He was killed around the 1340s BCE, and his sons continued his policy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/labayu.jpg" alt="Labayu" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amarna_letter._Letter_from_Labayu_(ruler_of_Shechem)_to_the_Egyptian_Pharaoh_Amenhotep_III_or_his_son_Akhenaten._14th_century_BCE._From_Tell_el-Amarna,_Egypt._British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
